<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  globalsettings.url.fragment.config - the ecommerce URL provider activation block
  (Foundry #1281, owner decision 2026-09-17).

  THIS FILE IS A FRAGMENT, NOT A SETTINGS FILE. It is staged to Files/System/Truvio/ and
  read by nobody at runtime. It exists because the block below has to be MERGED into the
  host's own Files/GlobalSettings.config, and a layer's files/ overlay is overwrite-wins
  with no merge step: shipping a whole GlobalSettings.config from a layer would replace the
  host's database connection, mail server and licence configuration. So the distribution
  ships the exact block, in a file whose only job is to be copied out of.

  WHAT IT FIXES
  /Globalsettings/System/Url/Providers is EMPTY on a stock Dynamicweb 10 install: friendly
  URLs for ecommerce are OPT-IN, and nothing turns them on. With the element empty, every
  product and group link a Swift storefront renders is a querystring - the PLP links and the
  PDP canonical both read /en-us/shop?GroupID=TCGRP-BUNDLES&ProductID=TCPROD0041 - and no
  /en-us/shop/<group>/<product> URL resolves at all. Measured on foundry.mydwsite4.com
  (DW 10.28.10, Swift 2.4.0, edition swift-demo). Reference solutions on the same box that
  DO render friendly product URLs carry exactly these providers in that element.
  UseCanonicalInEcommerce and IncludeProductIdInUrlNames are already True by default and are
  NOT set here; the providers are the missing half.

  The three providers below are the ecommerce ones only:
    eComGroupPathProvider           - the group path segment(s)
    eComProductProvider             - the product segment
    eComProductAndVariantProvider   - the product+variant segment
  The reference solutions also carry NewsItemProvider and ForumUrlProvider. Those belong to
  modules this distribution does not ship, so they are deliberately absent here - and the
  harness merges this element as a UNION, so a host that already carries them keeps them.

  HOW TO APPLY IT
  1. Open the host's Files/GlobalSettings.config.
  2. Merge the Providers element below into /Globalsettings/System/Url - merge, never
     replace: Providers is a comma-separated list and a host may already carry providers
     this distribution knows nothing about. Union the lists; drop nothing. If there is no
     <System><Url> branch at all, add it as a child of <Globalsettings>.
     The Foundry harness does this automatically (tools/harness/GlobalSettings.Fragment.ps1,
     allowlisted leaf Globalsettings/System/Url/Providers, merge rule comma-union).
  3. RESTART the host. The setting is read at application start; a running host keeps
     rendering querystring URLs.

  HOW TO PROVE IT
  Not by reading the setting back. Open the rendered PLP (/en-us/shop) and read a product
  link out of the HTML: PASS is a path URL with no ProductID= query, and the PDP canonical
  is that same path URL.

  CAVEAT - RENAMES DO NOT MINT A REDIRECT (Foundry #112)
  Once friendly ecommerce URLs are live, renaming a group or product regenerates the URL and
  NO 301 is minted for the old one. Any page list, link or bookmark pinned to a friendly
  product URL is invalidated by a rename, silently.

  WHY THE BASE CARRIES IT
  URL shape is not a feature, it is how every composed storefront addresses its catalogue:
  the PLP links, the PDP canonical, and every gate page-list pointer. A feature layer cannot
  own it, because an edition that drops the feature would silently drop friendly URLs from
  the whole storefront. It is configuration, not custom code, so it stays inside the base
  layer's zero-custom-code constraint.
-->
<Globalsettings>
  <System>
    <Url>
      <Providers>Dynamicweb.Frontend.UrlProviders.eComGroupPathProvider,Dynamicweb.Frontend.UrlProviders.eComProductProvider,Dynamicweb.Frontend.UrlProviders.eComProductAndVariantProvider</Providers>
    </Url>
  </System>
</Globalsettings>
